<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aden Protectorate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as British Aden Protectorate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> was the group of sultanates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> and sheikhdoms in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> bound to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by treaties of protection around the port of Aden. Its border with Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was fixed between 1904 and 1914, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> of Yemen later claimed it. Most of its states joined the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> before independence as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate.jpg" alt="Aden Protectorate" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lubiesque · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aden_Colony_dependencies.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> grew in the hinterland of the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and in the Hadhramaut after the Bombay Presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> conquered Aden in January 1839. British expansion there was meant to secure the port, which was then governed from British India. It began with informal arrangements of protection with nine states of the immediate hinterland: Abdali, Alawi, Amiri, Aqrabi, Aulaqi, Fadhli, Haushabi, Subeihi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yafa">Yafa</a>. From 1874 these arrangements existed with the tacit acceptance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which kept suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to the north, and the nine polities became known as the Nine Tribes or Nine Cantons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The British began to formalise protection with a treaty with the Mahra Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qishn">Qishn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socotra">Socotra</a> in 1886. More than thirty major treaties followed, the last signed in 1954, together with minor agreements. In exchange for protection the rulers agreed not to make treaties with, or cede territory to, any other foreign power. In 1917 control passed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> to the Foreign Office, and in 1937 the territory was divided for administration into the Western Protectorate, with a Political Officer at Lahej, and the Eastern Protectorate, with one at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukalla">Mukalla</a>.</p><p>From 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> signed advisory treaties with the Qu'aiti sultan and later with twelve other states, which allowed a Resident Advisor in each. The treaties stabilised the rulers' status and succession but encouraged official corruption, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aerial-bombardment">aerial bombardment</a> and collective punishment were sometimes used against tribes that defied the rulers. King Ahmad bin Yahya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, who did not recognise British suzerainty and wished to form a Greater Yemen, was involved in skirmishes along the Violet Line, a demarcation made in 1914.</p><p>On 11 February 1959 six rulers signed an accord forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-arab-emirates-of-the-south">Federation of Arab Emirates of the South</a>. Nine more joined, and on 18 January 1963 Aden Colony merged with it to make the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, while the states outside, mostly eastern, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of South Arabia. A state of emergency was declared on 10 December 1963. Two rival groups, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-liberation-of-occupied-south-yemen">Front for the Liberation of Occupied South Yemen</a>, fought the British and each other, and the British left Aden by the end of November 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Rulers kept a large degree of autonomy. Their flags flew over government buildings, they governed in their own names, and their states kept a distinct international personality, unlike the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. The boundaries and number of polities changed over time, and some, such as the Mahra Sultanate, barely had a functioning administration. The territory now forms part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aden Protectorate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Britain had held Aden since 1839 and built it into a major port and military base, while the sultanates, emirates and sheikhdoms of its hinterland formed the <b>Aden Protectorate</b> under British treaties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Yahya kept Yemen isolated, built a small army and claimed the <b>British Aden Protectorate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a></li><li><blockquote>Treaties with Britain from 1882 made the state part of the Aden Protectorate, later the Eastern <b>Aden Protectorate</b>, and the Kathiri-Qu'aiti agreement of 1918 settled relations with the Kathiri Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/quaiti-sultanate">Qu'aiti Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottomans introduced telegraphs, schools and courts and drew a border with the <b>British Aden Protectorate</b> in 1904-1914.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-vilayet">Yemen Vilayet</a></li></ul></section>
